{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "q02",
  "speechText": "In our system, we don't use only one type of search. We combine lexical search and semantic search. That's because lexical search is good for exact keywords, and semantic search is better for meaning and similar context. In practice, the lexical side uses PostgreSQL full-text search, and the semantic side uses embeddings with pgvector cosine similarity. Then we combine the two rankings with Reciprocal Rank Fusion, or RRF, so a result that is strong in both searches can move higher in the final ranking. We also think about failure and security. If the embedding service is unavailable, we can fall back to lexical results, and every search is still limited by the user's access. So the goal is to make retrieval more balanced, reliable, and safe instead of depending on vector search alone.",
